From 11df14c662504ca0e7c9b29b690ddfd641b49e3c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logdog2325 <loganjayfisher@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 12:26:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Hosted sessions never sync an imported save

Netplay ships the host's GBA saves to every client -- privacy-neutral for a
year of bundled templates, and a complete-save disclosure the moment 1.3.0 let
hosts import real files: boxes, inventory, progress, secret ID, all of it, on
the joiner's disk for the session. Community review caught it precisely.

Now, when a session starts on a machine whose port save is a user import, that
save is stashed and a DISPOSABLE stands in for it, built by the same party-
bundle machinery guests submit through: the bundled template plus exactly the
import's party and trainer identity -- what a battle reveals anyway. The
originals return when the session ends, restore rides the same deferred
crash-safe pattern as the team cleanup, first-stash-wins protects the import
if a session dies halfway, and a failed stash refuses hosting rather than
leaving a truncated copy a later heal would spread. Solo boots still play the
real save; that is what importing is for.

The team editor now locks whole while a room is open: its host-slot save was a
side door that would have written the full import back over the disposable
mid-room, and the next start would have synced exactly the file this patch
exists to keep off the wire.

Wording made honest on both platforms: importing shares a rebuilt party-and-
identity save when hosting, never the full file; and the guest bundle note
says what it actually discloses partner-side, down to the secret trainer ID.

This completes Akiak's disposable-save design on the host side.

Riding along, because it touches the same window code: the desktop app now
calls itself OrreLink everywhere a user can see -- OrreLink.app on macOS,
OrreLink.exe on Windows, an OrreLink binary on Linux, and a window title of
"OrreLink <version>" instead of a git revision string.
